So the way to think about this is: a **class** is like an architectural blueprint for a house. It says *what fields exist*, *what methods can be called*, and *how to construct it* — but it doesn't occupy any real memory on its own. An **object** is the actual house you build using that blueprint. When you write `Car myCar = new Car();`, the `Car` class is the blueprint and `myCar` is the actual object living on the **heap**.

```java
class Car {
    String brand;  // field
    void drive() { System.out.println("Driving"); } // method
}

Car myCar = new Car();  // object created on heap
myCar.brand = "Toyota";
```

You can create **multiple objects** from the same class — each gets its own copy of instance fields, but they all share the same method definitions.

This is called **aliasing** — when two or more references point to the same object. It's a common source of subtle bugs.

```java
List<String> a = new ArrayList<>();
a.add("Hello");

List<String> b = a;  // b is an ALIAS for the same list
b.add("World");

System.out.println(a); // [Hello, World] — a sees the change!
```

Both `a` and `b` refer to the exact same `ArrayList` on the heap. When `b.add("World")` runs, it modifies the shared object — so `a` sees it too.

This is why **immutable objects** (like `String`, `Integer`) are safer in many contexts — you can't accidentally modify the shared state. If you want a truly independent copy, you need to explicitly clone or copy the object.

A well-structured Java class typically has these components:

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // 1. Fields (state)
    private String owner;
    private double balance;
    private static int totalAccounts = 0; // static field

    // 2. Static initializer block
    static { System.out.println("Class loaded"); }

    // 3. Constructor (initialization)
    public BankAccount(String owner, double balance) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balance = balance;
        totalAccounts++;
    }

    // 4. Methods (behavior)
    public void deposit(double amount) { balance += amount; }
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
}
```

Each component has a specific role: fields store state, constructors set up the object when `new` is called, methods define operations, and static members are class-level shared resources.
